"A bacterium is one whole cell. It has a wall, a membrane, and its own DNA, though it has no nucleus to keep the DNA in. It takes in food and grows. When it is big enough, it copies its DNA and splits in two. In the best conditions, some bacteria can do this about every 20 minutes.": "da22505ab35d",
"A virus is much simpler and much smaller. It is a short set of instructions wrapped in a coat of protein. Most viruses are about a hundred times smaller than a bacterium. A virus cannot take in food, and it cannot grow. Left alone, it does nothing at all.": "19ce69fd53bf",
"A virus is much smaller than a cell.": "eb9b7dd7b9bc",
"Bacteria live almost everywhere: in soil, in water, in hot springs, and deep in rock. They live on your skin and inside you too. Your body carries about as many bacterial cells as it has cells of its own. Most of them do no harm, and many are useful. Other bacteria turn milk into yogurt. Only some kinds cause disease.": "406a2bce6213",
