"Most bacteria do us no harm.": "68b5f5062673",
"People call bacteria and viruses germs.": "cc0a91f82a42",
"People often use one word, germs, for both bacteria and viruses. Both are far too small to see, and some of each can make us sick. But they are not two kinds of the same thing. One is a living cell. The other is not a cell at all.": "df4671044820",
"People often use one word, germs, for both bacteria and viruses. Both are far too small to see, and some of each can make us sick. But they are not two kinds of the same thing. One is a living cell. The other is not a cell at all. A bacterium is one whole cell. It has a wall, a membrane, and its own DNA, though it has no nucleus to keep the DNA in. It takes in food and grows. When it is big enough, it copies its DNA and splits in two. In the best conditions, some bacteria can do this about every 20 minutes. Bacteria live almost everywhere: in soil, in water, in hot springs, and deep in rock. They live on your skin and inside you too. Your body carries about as many bacterial cells as it has cells of its own. Most of them do no harm, and many are useful. Other bacteria turn milk into yogurt. Only some kinds cause disease. A virus is much simpler and much smaller. It is a short set of instructions wrapped in a coat of protein. Most viruses are about a hundred times smaller than a bacterium. A virus cannot take in food, and it cannot grow. Left alone, it does nothing at all. To make more viruses, a virus has to get inside a living cell. Once its instructions are in, the cell follows them and builds new viruses out of its own materials. The new viruses then leave and find other cells. There are viruses for every kind of living thing, even for bacteria. So is a virus alive? Scientists do not all agree. It carries instructions and it changes over time, as living things do, but it cannot do anything by itself. The difference matters in medicine. Antibiotics are medicines that stop bacteria from growing or kill them. They do not work on viruses, such as the ones that cause a cold.": "f8ee95a40eb1",
"So is a virus alive? Scientists do not all agree. It carries instructions and it changes over time, as living things do, but it cannot do anything by itself. The difference matters in medicine. Antibiotics are medicines that stop bacteria from growing or kill them. They do not work on viruses, such as the ones that cause a cold.": "d926e66a6216",
